The within-pool slope ($-$0.401, CI excludes zero) is statistically indistinguishable from the independent 16-relation pool ($-$0.509; permutation $p \approx 0.60$). The smaller quartile gap ($-$3.92\,pp vs.\ $-$5.31\,pp) reflects the narrower BCS range within the 80-relation pool (Appendix~\ref{sec:appendix_within_pool}).

In the full 80-relation condition, natural BCS variation is insufficient (IQR${}=0.033$) to yield a detectable effect ($n=25$, slope${}=+0.221$, $p \approx 0.76$). This does not invalidate the confusability effect---the within-pool slope is significant ($p=0.027$) when BCS is deliberately varied---but establishes that natural schema sampling at this scale does not produce sufficient BCS contrast for detection.

\section{SCS Embedding Ablation: Name-Only vs. Name+Definition}
\label{app:scs-ablation}

Table~\ref{tab:scs-ablation} compares predictive accuracy of name-only SCS against name+definition SCS across three datasets.

\begin{table}[h]
\centering
\small
\begin{tabular}{lcccc}
\toprule
Dataset & SCS$_\text{name}$ $r$ & SCS$_\text{def}$ $r$ & $\Delta r$ & Matrix corr. \\
\midrule
Few-NERD & 0.172 & 0.149 & $-$0.023 & 0.894 \\
MAVEN & 0.129 & 0.164 & +0.035 & 0.656 \\
FewRel & 0.178 & 0.193 & +0.015 & 0.848 \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\caption{Pearson correlation between SCS and empirical confusion rate. ``Def'' concatenates type name with definition before embedding. All correlations significant ($p < 0.001$). The inconsistent direction of $\Delta r$ indicates no systematic benefit from including definitions.}
\label{tab:scs-ablation}
\end{table}

Few-NERD is the only dataset whose prompts include type definitions; for MAVEN and FewRel, prompts use type names alone, making name-only SCS a direct match to model input. The high matrix correlation (0.66--0.89) confirms that type names capture the dominant semantic structure.

\section{Pair-to-Batch Aggregation Simulation}
\label{app:aggregation-sim}

We verify that the pair-level $R^2 \approx 7\%$ (computed over non-zero confusion pairs) can aggregate to the observed batch-level $R^2 \approx 22\%$ via a Monte Carlo simulation.

\paragraph{Setup.} From the FewRel 16-relation experiment, we estimate the inter-pair SCS correlation for pairs sharing a common type ($\rho_\text{SCS} = 0.35$). For batches of $k=8$ types ($\binom{8}{2}=28$ pairs), we simulate 10{,}000 iterations: (1) generate 28 correlated pair-level effects with individual $R^2 = 0.074$; (2) average to obtain batch-level signals; (3) compute batch-level $R^2$.

\paragraph{Results.} Simulated batch $R^2 = 0.219$ [95\% CI: 0.092--0.361], matching the observed value of 0.218. The effective amplification factor is $\sim$3$\times$, explained by signal correlation within batches preserving variance while independent noise averages out (effective degrees of freedom $\approx 3.4$, not 28).

\end{document}

\section{Discussion}
\label{sec:discussion}

\paragraph{Boundary conditions.}
The two-channel model applies to cross-domain schemas; same-domain schemas (e.g., MAVEN's FrameNet-derived event types) exhibit centrality confounding that obscures per-type aggregated signals, requiring pairwise SCS or centrality adjustment (\S\ref{sec:centrality_confound}). High-BCS accuracy converges to $\approx$51\% across tested models, suggesting a task-inherent difficulty ceiling. RE's clean channel separation may partly reflect its closed-set format; the effect generalizes to document-level RE (DocRED slope $-$0.71). Prompt design modulates but does not eliminate the effect (v2 gap: $-$25.5\,pp vs.\ v1: $-$31.2\,pp; Appendix~\ref{sec:appendix_prompt_sensitivity}).

\paragraph{Why does embedding similarity predict LLM confusion?}
Type name embeddings capture semantic proximity in the same representational space that LLMs use for in-context type resolution. When two type names are close in embedding space, we hypothesize that the model's attention mechanism may produce overlapping key--query representations for those types, increasing the probability of substitution errors. This mechanistic link explains why a simple cosine similarity metric predicts extraction failures: high SCS directly indexes the degree of representational overlap that causes confusion during decoding. Critically, the pair-level effect is modest ($R^2 \approx 7\%$ over non-zero confusion pairs) because individual substitution events are stochastic and influenced by many factors beyond type similarity. However, pairs sharing a common type exhibit correlated SCS values ($\rho = 0.35$), so when multiple high-SCS pairs co-occur in a batch, their effects aggregate constructively: noise averages out while the confusability signal is preserved. Monte Carlo simulation confirms that this inter-pair correlation produces an effective amplification of ${\sim}3\times$, yielding batch-level $R^2 \approx 22\%$ from pair-level $R^2 \approx 7\%$ (Appendix~\ref{app:aggregation-sim}). This aggregation mechanism explains why weak individual pair effects produce strong batch-level degradation---and why BCS (the mean pairwise SCS) is a useful schema-level diagnostic despite the modest pairwise signal.

\paragraph{Schema design implications.}
Our findings suggest a pre-hoc workflow for practitioners designing extraction schemas. Before deployment, one can compute all-pairs SCS across the type inventory and flag pairs exceeding a high percentile (e.g., 75th) of the corpus's SCS distribution as high-risk for mutual confusion. Three mitigation strategies follow from the two-channel model:
(i)~\emph{Expand definitions}---for high-SCS pairs, include contrastive definitions or disambiguating examples in the prompt to increase representational separation;
(ii)~\emph{Rename types}---replace semantically proximate labels with more descriptive alternatives (e.g., ``person-artist'' $\to$ ``visual artist (painter/sculptor)'') to reduce embedding overlap;
(iii)~\emph{Decompose batches}---assign high-SCS pairs to different extraction passes using graph coloring on the SCS-thresholded confusability graph (\S\ref{sec:sdgd}), accepting the precision--recall trade-off that merge introduces.
We deliberately avoid recommending a fixed SCS threshold, as optimal granularity is task-dependent: the acceptable confusion rate depends on downstream costs, schema size, and available compute budget for multi-pass extraction.

\paragraph{Cross-domain connections.}
Schema complexity effects may parallel findings in schema-guided dialogue systems (\S2; see Appendix~\ref{app:cross-domain} for discussion).

\paragraph{Model scale and confusability sensitivity.}
Cross-model validation with Llama-3.1-8B-Instruct provides a preliminary view of how model capacity interacts with both degradation channels. Channel~1 (count-driven decline) replicates fully: Few-NERD recall drops from 0.70 to 0.27 on the 8B model versus 0.72 to 0.42 on Qwen3-32B (Table~\ref{tab:cross_model}). Channel~2 (confusability) replicates on RE, where the 8B model's BCS gap ($-$27.1\,pp) reaches 87\% of the 32B gap ($-$31.2\,pp; Table~\ref{tab:cross_model_bcs}). On NER, however, the 8B model exhibits floor effects: accuracy in high-count conditions is already near chance, compressing the gradient available for confusability to modulate. We interpret this as a capacity boundary---structured NER output generation with many types overwhelms the 8B model's decoder, producing uniform failures that mask the subtler confusability signal. Speculatively, larger models may attenuate Channel~2 effect sizes through superior representational capacity for distinguishing similar types, but are unlikely to eliminate the effect entirely, since embedding similarity reflects genuine semantic overlap in the type inventory. We emphasize that $n{=}2$ models constitutes a cross-model consistency check rather than a broad generalization claim; proprietary model coverage remains a limitation (Limitations).

\paragraph{Future directions.}
Three directions merit investigation: (i)~non-oracle routing strategies (e.g., embedding-based instance-to-batch assignment) that would make decomposition deployable without gold labels; (ii)~broader model coverage, particularly proprietary APIs; and (iii)~definition-inclusive SCS that incorporates type descriptions rather than names alone. Current name+definition ablation shows no systematic improvement (Appendix~\ref{app:scs-ablation}); future work could explore weighted combinations or contextualized embeddings that better capture definition semantics. For NER, merge amplification blocks naive decomposition (\S\ref{sec:sdgd}); calibration-aware merge, joint decoding, or coarse-to-fine extraction may address this.

\section{Conclusion}
\label{sec:conclusion}

We isolated schema-induced degradation into count-driven recall decline and confusability-driven type confusion, showed these channels are separable in RE, with Channel~2 additionally impairing entity detection in NER, and confirmed that both channels replicate across model families on RE (Meta-Llama-3.1-8B-Instruct: count $r = 0.725$, confusability $\Delta = -27$\,pp) and across extraction granularities (DocRED document-level RE: count decline $-$49\,pp; BCS slope $= -0.71$, $R^2 = 0.53$), with high-BCS accuracy converging to a floor consistent across both tested models. We further identified a centrality confound on same-domain schemas, demonstrated that merge amplification is a structural challenge for NER decomposition, and showed that confusability-aware grouping yields a directionally consistent but non-significant improvement (+1.4\,pp, $p\approx 0.19$). Cross-batch calibration or joint decoding for NER, and broader validation of RE batch composition, are the key next steps.

\section{Schema Decomposition: Failure, Diagnosis, and Mitigation}
\label{sec:sdgd}

A natural intervention against confusability-driven degradation is to partition the schema so that confusable types never co-occur in the same prompt. We test this idea with \textbf{Schema-DAG-Guided Decomposition} (SDGD\footnote{SDGD (Schema Decomposition and Grouping for Degradation mitigation) is an internal project designation; the graph structure is undirected.}), which uses greedy graph coloring on the pairwise SCS graph to assign types to low-confusability batches.

We construct a thresholded confusability graph $G = (V, E)$ where each type is a vertex and an edge connects types $t_i, t_j$ whenever $\text{SCS}(t_i, t_j) > \tau$. Greedy graph coloring (\textsc{largest\_first}) assigns each type to a color such that no two adjacent---i.e., highly confusable---types share a batch. Each color class forms one extraction batch. For NER, predictions from all batches are merged via set union; for RE, each instance is routed to the batch containing its gold type (oracle routing). We test four NER modes: (i)~\textbf{single\_pass}, the undecomposed baseline; (ii)~\textbf{sdgd\_zero}, SCS-guided partitioning; (iii)~\textbf{random\_batch}, random partitioning with matched batch sizes; and (iv)~\textbf{per\_type}, each type extracted in isolation. Every mode falls below the single-pass NER baseline: SDGD obtains F1\,=\,0.251, random batching 0.320, per-type 0.292, versus 0.445 for single-pass (Table~\ref{tab:sdgd_results}, Appendix~\ref{sec:appendix_sdgd}).

\paragraph{Merge amplification in NER.}
The core failure mechanism is \emph{merge amplification}: presenting the same text to the model $k$ times with different type subsets causes each pass to independently generate entity predictions, and union merge accumulates all of them into a single output. Each pass produces an independent set of span predictions; because LLM outputs are not calibrated across passes \cite{llm-calibration-survey-2024}, the same span may receive conflicting type labels from different passes, or hallucinated entities may appear in passes where the correct type is absent. Union merge retains all predictions, so false positives scale with $k$ while true positives are bounded by the fixed set of gold entities.

Quantitatively, single-pass precision (0.563) collapses to 0.210--0.249 after union merge across 8 batches, while recall gains are modest (0.368 $\to$ 0.314 for sdgd\_zero, 0.446 for random batch). The net F1 loss is substantial: 0.445 $\to$ 0.251 (sdgd\_zero) or 0.320 (random batch). Per-type extraction (66 batches) pushes recall to 0.586 but precision to 0.194 (F1\,=\,0.292), confirming that the precision--recall imbalance intensifies with the number of passes. Random batching outperforms confusability-aware SDGD on NER (0.320 vs.\ 0.251) because the dominant degradation source---merge amplification---is independent of batch composition; any multi-pass scheme suffers from it regardless of grouping quality.

\paragraph{Confidence gating does not resolve merge amplification.}
We tested a confidence-gated variant (v2) that ranks competing predictions by the LLM's self-reported confidence and retains only the highest-scoring candidate per span. Confidence gating improves precision (sdgd\_zero: 0.210$\to$0.324; random\_batch: 0.249$\to$0.331) but causes disproportionate recall loss (0.314$\to$0.191; 0.446$\to$0.276), yielding lower F1 in both modes (sdgd\_zero: 0.251$\to$0.241; random\_batch: 0.320$\to$0.301). This indicates that LLM confidence scores are insufficiently calibrated to distinguish correct predictions from hallucinated ones across independent passes, so confidence gating discards true and false positives at comparable rates.

\paragraph{Why NER fails but RE does not.}
Merge amplification is specific to span-level tasks. Each NER pass independently proposes entity spans, and union merge must resolve conflicting boundaries and type assignments, degrading systematically with pass count. RE avoids this mechanism entirely: under oracle routing, each relation instance receives a single classification from the batch containing its gold type, requiring no span reconciliation and eliminating the multi-pass interference that causes SDGD to fail on NER. This makes RE a clean testbed for evaluating whether confusability-aware decomposition improves accuracy once merge artifacts are removed.

\paragraph{Oracle routing as performance ceiling.}
Oracle routing routes each test instance to the batch containing its gold type, eliminating cross-type confusion within each batch. On FewRel 80-relation RE, oracle-routed decomposition achieves 0.802 accuracy versus 0.504 for the single-pass baseline---a gain of +29.8\,pp (Table~\ref{tab:batch_composition}). This is the strongest positive finding of the decomposition experiments: it demonstrates that type confusability is indeed a key driver of degradation in RE, and that performance recovers substantially when confusable types are separated.

However, oracle routing requires gold labels and is not deployable. The gap between the oracle ceiling and practical routing reveals an important asymmetry: while \emph{any} decomposition benefits enormously from removing the 80-type bottleneck (+28.4\,pp for random batching, +29.8\,pp for graph coloring), the \emph{additional} benefit of confusability-aware grouping over random grouping is modest (+1.4\,pp, $p \approx 0.19$). The confusability-aware strategy ranks 13th out of 15 random seeds, limited by the narrow BCS margin (0.006) between graph-coloring and random batches at this partition granularity. Finer-grained partitioning or alternative confusability metrics may be needed to translate the oracle-demonstrated potential into significant practical gains.

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\small
\begin{tabular}{@{}lcc@{}}
\toprule
\textbf{Condition} & \textbf{Accuracy} & \textbf{Mean BCS} \\
\midrule
Single-pass (80 types) & 0.504 & 0.557 \\
Random batching (15 seeds)$^\dagger$ & 0.788\,$\pm$\,0.018 & 0.559 \\
Confus.-aware (graph coloring) & \textbf{0.802} & 0.552 \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\caption{Batch composition on FewRel 80-relation RE with oracle routing. $^\dagger$Mean $\pm$ SD across 15 random seeds. Graph coloring yields +1.4\,pp over random batching ($p \approx 0.19$, n.s.) with a narrow BCS margin (0.006). The +29.8\,pp gap between single-pass and decomposed conditions confirms that type separation recovers performance when merge amplification is absent.}
\label{tab:batch_composition}
\end{table}

